# Local-loopback HTTP proofs

Download this `.livemd` file, open it in [Livebook](https://livebook.dev), and choose
**Open → Import from file**, then **Run all**. Use Elixir 1.18 or later and OTP 27 or later
with access to public Hex. No secrets or external services are needed.

Select the local HTTP application profile, sign a request to a literal loopback address,
and refuse a wrong nonce, the standard proof profile, and a hostname target.

The host derives the target from its listener and requires its exact server nonce. Loopback
HTTP provides neither TLS nor isolation between local processes. Verification grants no
execution authority. This notebook makes no HTTP calls and creates no listener or files.

[Open this 1.5.0 release notebook](https://livebook.dev/run?url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fbaselabs%2Fbounded_authority_protocol%2Fblob%2Fbaselabs_bounded_authority_protocol-v1.5.0%2Fdocs%2Fnotebooks%2Flocal-loopback.livemd).
The launch opens this journey at its immutable release tag.

## Setup

Install the exact published package. Fixed evaluation times keep the results reproducible;
only safe semantic values are persisted, never keys, signatures, tokens, or nonces.

```elixir
Mix.install([{:bounded_authority_protocol, "== 1.5.0"}])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:ok
```

## Generate issuer and holder keys

The application profile uses Ed25519 keys and a contract-major 1 grant. Generate only the
keys needed for that grant and its local proof.

```elixir
{issuer_public, issuer_private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519)
{holder_public, holder_private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519)

{:ok, holder_thumbprint} =
  BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.Jwk.public_key_thumbprint_raw(holder_public, %{})

:keys_ready
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:keys_ready
```

## Build and sign a local-tool grant

Use a host-pinned audience and a local-tool operation. The grant is standard contract-major 1;
only the application proof uses the local-loopback profile.

```elixir
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.{Grant, Operation}

grant = %Grant{
  key_id: "issuer-1",
  issuer: "https://issuer.example",
  grant_id: "grant-2026-001",
  audiences: ["urn:example:local-tools"],
  issued_at: 1_800_000_000,
  not_before: 1_800_000_000,
  expires_at: 1_800_003_600,
  holder_thumbprint: holder_thumbprint,
  operations: [
    %Operation{
      name: "run_local_tool",
      selectors: [:all]
    }
  ]
}

{:ok, signing_input} = BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.grant_signing_input(grant, %{})
issuer_signature = :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :none, signing_input.message, [issuer_private, :ed25519])

{:ok, grant_compact} =
  BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.assemble_compact(signing_input, issuer_signature)

:grant_signed
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:grant_signed
```

## Normalize the target and sign the local proof

The profile accepts a canonical literal-loopback HTTP target and requires a nonce. The
standard HTTPS proof producer does not serve this listener.

```elixir
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.ApplicationProfile.LocalLoopbackHttp.V1, as: LocalLoopback
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.{Proof, Credentials, ExpectedRequest, TrustedIssuer}

now = 1_800_001_000

loopback_target = "http://127.0.0.1:4000/invoke"
loopback_nonce = "challenge-loopback-001"

{:ok, ^loopback_target} = LocalLoopback.normalize_uri(loopback_target, %{})

loopback_proof = %Proof{
  holder_public_key: holder_public,
  proof_id: "proof-loopback-2026-001",
  method: "POST",
  target_uri: loopback_target,
  issued_at: now,
  nonce: loopback_nonce,
  invocation_id: "7d444840-9dc0-11ed-a8fc-0242ac120003",
  operation: "run_local_tool",
  grant_compact: grant_compact,
  cast_arguments: {:object, [{"tool", {:string, "index_workspace"}}]}
}

{:ok, loopback_input} = LocalLoopback.proof_signing_input(loopback_proof, %{})

loopback_signature =
  :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :none, loopback_input.message, [holder_private, :ed25519])

{:ok, loopback_compact} =
  LocalLoopback.assemble_compact(loopback_input, loopback_signature)

:local_proof_signed
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:local_proof_signed
```

## Verify the local envelope

Choose the application-profile verifier explicitly. Require the exact listener-derived target
and server-issued challenge, then show only the operation and non-authorization marker.

```elixir
trusted = %TrustedIssuer{key_id: "issuer-1", public_key: issuer_public}

loopback_credentials = %Credentials{grant: grant_compact, proof: loopback_compact}

loopback_expected = %ExpectedRequest{
  trusted_issuer: trusted,
  issuer: "https://issuer.example",
  audience: "urn:example:local-tools",
  method: "POST",
  target_uri: loopback_target,
  invocation_id: "7d444840-9dc0-11ed-a8fc-0242ac120003",
  operation: "run_local_tool",
  cast_arguments: loopback_proof.cast_arguments,
  evaluation_time: now,
  clock_skew: 60,
  proof_max_age: 300,
  nonce: {:required, loopback_nonce},
  bounds: %{}
}

{:ok, loopback_facts} =
  LocalLoopback.check_envelope(loopback_credentials, loopback_expected)

Map.take(loopback_facts, [:target_uri, :operation, :authorization])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{target_uri: "http://127.0.0.1:4000/invoke", operation: "run_local_tool", authorization: :not_evaluated}
```

## Refuse a different server nonce

A proof carrying a nonce is insufficient: it must match the challenge the host expected.

```elixir
{:error, :invalid} =
  LocalLoopback.check_envelope(loopback_credentials, %{
    loopback_expected
    | nonce: {:required, "wrong-nonce"}
  })
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:error, :invalid}
```

## Refuse profile fallback and hostname targets

Standard envelope verification refuses the local proof. `localhost` is also refused without
DNS lookup; a listener must supply its literal loopback address.

```elixir
{:error, :invalid} =
  BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.check_envelope(loopback_credentials, loopback_expected)

{:error, :invalid} = LocalLoopback.normalize_uri("http://localhost:4000/invoke", %{})

[:standard_profile, :hostname]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[:standard_profile, :hostname]
```

See [framework and runtime integration](../guides/framework-integration.md) for host request
boundaries and [nonce policy](../guides/nonce-and-time-policy.md) for challenge ownership.
